Õ”È˜pÅá*B]“Zøõ_t√≥“Ä◊á4Ÿô,ù{"url":"/gcsl/language/records-and-types/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/language/records-and-types/","content":"Records and Types. A record type describes the fields a value supplies. GCSL uses structural types: two declarations with the same structure describe the same type, and a record can be used where fewer of its fields are required. Declaring a shape # Use type to give a structure a reusable name. An annotation checks a value against that structure. Defaults supply omitted fields in a record literal checked against the type: Copytype Install = {from: String, exclude: List<String> = []} let item: Install = {from = \"src\"} item.exclude Defaults are constant expressions, not arbitrary function calls. They are applied where a record literal has the expected type; they don't add fields to an already constructed variable with a smaller type. An annotation also determines what fields later code can see. Extra fields in a record literal produce a warning when an expected type hides them. A structural alias does not introduce a distinct runtime identity. Generic types # A type can take type parameters, named in angle brackets after its name. Each use names the types to put in their place: Copytype Pair<A, B> = {first: A, second: B} let entry: Pair<String, Int> = {first = \"port\"; second = 8080} entry.second A use must give as many type arguments as the type has parameters, and a type without parameters takes none. Because types are structural, Pair<String, Int> is the same type as {first: String, second: Int}. A generic type can be built from another, as in type Named<T> = Pair<String, T>. It can't refer to itself, and it can't spread one of its parameters, because the parameter might not be a record. Enums # An extensible enum is a type whose variants any module can add. Declare the enum, with an optional bound that every variant's payload must fit, and then its variants, each with a payload type: Copyenum Shape extensible<{*: Int}> enum Shape::Square<{side: Int}> enum Shape::Point square = Shape::Square({side = 3}) A variant is also a value: Shape::Square on its own has the type Variant<Shape, {side: Int}>. Calling it makes a value of the enum, and you can pass it to a function as a key. kind.matches(value) tests whether a value is of that variant, and kind.payload(value) takes out its payload: Copyenum Tool extensible enum Tool::C<{cc: String}> fn find<T>(kind: Variant<Tool, T>, tools: List<Tool>, fallback: T): T { for t in tools { if kind.matches(t) { return kind.payload(t) } } fallback } cc = find(Tool::C, [Tool::C({cc = \"gcc\"})], {cc = \"cc\"}).cc Unlike records, enums are nominal: two enums are different types even if they look alike. value is Tool tests whether a value is of the enum. Values of an enum are compared with == only when the enum has a bound, because an unbounded payload might hold a function. A variant is visible in its own file, to the other files of its directory and the directories below it, and to every file that imports its module. Partial records # Partial<T> holds any of the fields of the record type T. Use it to describe a record by only some of its fields, and .matches() to test a whole record against it: Copytype Platform = {arch: String, os: String} let wanted: Partial<Platform> = {arch = \"x86_64\"} fits = wanted.matches({arch = \"x86_64\"; os = \"linux\"}) A field of a partial record can't be read by name, because it may be absent. Records of any fields # A rest-field, *: T, gives the type of every field that a record type doesn't name. Use it when the field names are data, such as paths. A {*: String} holds any fields, each a String. Named fields keep their own types, so {to: String, *: Int} holds a String field to, and any other fields are Ints: Copylet files: {*: String} = {\"bin/*\" = \"bin/*\"; \"sbin/*\" = \"sbin/*\"} files Write the record literal where the type is expected: in an annotation, an argument or a definition's result. There, each field that the type doesn't name is checked against the rest-field. A value whose own type has no rest-field doesn't fit one, because a record keeps fields that its static type hides, and they could be of any type. Read a named field as usual. A field that only the rest-field covers can't be read by name, and a record whose type has a rest-field can't be spread. Typed equality compares every field, and is and as test each field that the rest-field covers. Combining fields # Dotted definitions construct nested records. Compatible dotted paths merge, including across files in a workspace: Copyserver.host = \"localhost\" server.port = 8080 server.tls.enabled = true A whole-field definition such as server = {...} closes that field to further dotted extensions. Define the individual dotted leaves when you want several files to contribute to a shared structure. A spread starts from another record's visible fields. Inside an ordinary record block, put defaults first and explicit overrides afterward: Copylet defaults = {host = \"localhost\"; port = 80} let config = { ...defaults port = 8080 } config.port A spread copies only fields visible through its operand's static type. It cannot replace an earlier explicit field inside an ordinary block. Top-level declarations are resolved across the whole input and take precedence over a spread of the same name regardless of textual order. Unions and narrowing # A union, such as Int | String, allows more than one shape. Use is to test a value and as for a checked conversion that fails at runtime if the value doesn't fit. In if name is Type, the branches see narrowed copies of the named value. This does not perform general flow analysis of later statements or dotted field paths. Typed equality == compares operands of the same static type. Total equality === compares complete runtime records, including fields hidden by their shared static type. Both require the same static operand type. The language's restrictions on unions, functions, and futures are described in gcsl(5) and Inference Limits.","word_count":994,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / The Language","title":"Records and Types"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"declaring-a-shape","text":"Declaring a shape #","location":39},{"element":"h2","id":"generic-types","text":"Generic types #","location":156},{"element":"h2","id":"enums","text":"Enums #","location":273},{"element":"h2","id":"partial-records","text":"Partial records #","location":489},{"element":"h2","id":"records-of-any-fields","text":"Records of any fields #","location":562},{"element":"h2","id":"combining-fields","text":"Combining fields #","location":741},{"element":"h2","id":"unions-and-narrowing","text":"Unions and narrowing #","location":882}]}},"word_data":{"declar":[{"position":17,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":39,"weight":144,"original_word":null},{"position":287,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":861,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"hold":[{"position":458,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":493,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":597,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":614,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"copyserv":[{"position":759,"weight":12,"original_word":null}],"shapepoint":[{"position":317,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"return":[{"position":397,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"valu":[{"position":10,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":56,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":328,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":343,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":358,"weight":8,"original_word":null},{"position":362,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":368,"weight":8,"original_word":null},{"position":427,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":433,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":438,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":669,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":903,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":916,"